# Testing Standards

## Test Structure (AAA Pattern)

```typescript
describe('UserService', () => {
  describe('createUser', () => {
    it('should create user with valid data', () => {
      // Arrange
      const data = { 
        email: 'test@example.com', 
        name: 'Test User' 
      };
      const mockDb = createMockDb();
      
      // Act
      const result = userService.createUser(data);
      
      // Assert
      expect(result).toBeDefined();
      expect(result.email).toBe(data.email);
      expect(mockDb.insert).toHaveBeenCalledWith('users', data);
    });
  });
});
```

## Test Naming Convention

```
should_[expectedBehavior]_when_[condition]
```

Examples:
- `should_return_user_when_id_exists`
- `should_throw_error_when_input_invalid`
- `should_create_order_when_payment_successful`

## Test Organization

```
tests/
├── unit/           # Fast, isolated tests
├── integration/    # API, database tests
└── e2e/            # Full user flows
```

## What to Test

| Type | Coverage |
|------|----------|
| Happy path | Expected behavior |
| Error cases | Exceptions, failures |
| Edge cases | Boundaries, empty input |
| Integration | Component interactions |

## Best Practices

```typescript
// One assertion per test (or related group)
it('should validate email format', () => {
  expect(isValidEmail('test@example.com')).toBe(true);
  expect(isValidEmail('invalid')).toBe(false);
});

// Use descriptive test names
it('should return 404 when user does not exist', async () => {
  // ...
});

// Mock external dependencies
jest.mock('./database');
```
